# NOTABLE CAMPAIGNS #

For its advertisement campaigns, Valentino has in the past worked with photographers including  [LINK: (Deborah Turbeville) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deborah_Turbeville]  (2011) [LINK: ([55]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valentino_(fashion_house)#cite_note-55]   [LINK: (Terry Richardson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terry_Richardson]  (2016), [LINK: ([56]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valentino_(fashion_house)#cite_note-56]   [LINK: (Inez and Vinoodh) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inez_and_Vinoodh]  (2020), [LINK: ([57]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valentino_(fashion_house)#cite_note-57]   [LINK: (Michael Bailey-Gates) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Bailey-Gates]  (2022) [LINK: ([58]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valentino_(fashion_house)#cite_note-58]  and  [LINK: (Glen Luchford) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glen_Luchford]  (2025). [LINK: ([59]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valentino_(fashion_house)#cite_note-59]

For Valentino's Spring/Summer 2016 women's ready-to-wear collection, which referenced African culture, sporting prints and motifs commonly seen across the continent, photographer  [LINK: (Steve McCurry) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_McCurry]  shot a campaign set against the backdrop of  [LINK: (Amboseli National Park) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amboseli_National_Park]  in Kenya and included local  [LINK: (Maasai people) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maasai_people] . The campaign sparked criticism on social media regarding perceived racial insensitivities. [LINK: ([60]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valentino_(fashion_house)#cite_note-60]


# LEGAL ISSUES #

In 2020, Valentino and  [LINK: (Amazon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazon_(company)]  filed a joint lawsuit against New York-based Kaitlyn Pan Group for allegedly counterfeiting Valentino's Rockstud shoes and offering them for sale online. [LINK: ([61]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valentino_(fashion_house)#cite_note-61]

Amid the  [LINK: (COVID-19 pandemic in the United States) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic_in_the_United_States] , Valentino sought in June 2020 to end the lease for its American flagship on New York's  [LINK: (Fifth Avenue) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fifth_Avenue]  nine years early, saying the pandemic made it impossible to run the store "consistent with the luxury, prestigious, high-quality reputation" of its neighbourhood. After a New York state trial judge dismissed that lawsuit, the landlord sued Valentino for $207.1 million, mainly to recover unpaid rent and to repair store damage. By 2023, Valentino settled litigation with the landlord. [LINK: ([62]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valentino_(fashion_house)#cite_note-62]


